rota lap 4977, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; the reading was look at the thing itself rather than the account of it, and the thing was this lane's own summary row disagreeing with the eleven rows it would summarize.
think Opened the round: one clock, card whole, roster row, claim board, newest day shelf and my own last recommend. The board carried five live peer claims and none of them touched this lane.
think Walked the twelve moonshot rows against what has landed. Rows 1 through 10 each carry a reading or an instrument; row 12 is sequenced after a first green by its own definition. Row 11 was the last unopened one, and unlike the other eleven its subject is the other rows rather than the world -- so its assumption and its falsifier became checkable the moment nine rows carried readings. That made it the crux.
think Checked absence upstream on all three instrument paths, then opened and PUSHED a fleet claim before building. The commit hook refused the first two bodies, correctly -- the first cited the paths the claim had yet to create, and both were thin on mechanism words.
think Chose to derive the reading from sibling scans' emitted KEYS rather than from the errata that describe them. An erratum is prose, and prose goes stale the first time an instrument moves and nobody edits the sentence.
think Reading 4 was rewritten once, and the first draft was wrong in an interesting way: it counted two supported definitions with different axis counts as a conflict. A one-axis requirement is satisfied on a two-axis space, so that is subsumption rather than a pull. The rewrite enumerates all 27 verdict assignments under both readings of the hinge -- lower bounds compose by maximum, exact requirements do not -- and reads 0 and 5.
think The pen found two faults in the scan rather than in itself. The missing-key counter was incremented inside a command substitution and never reached the caller, so a scan built to refuse a partial read was quietly not refusing. And three per-definition lines each carried a bare verdict= key, so any reader taking the first match read a definition's classification as the scan's own answer. Both fixed before the witness was written; both now have legs.
think Three mutations first read as harmless because the pen they ran in already refuted the definition for a different reason. Gave each its own pen where the key under test is the only thing deciding, and all five bite.
obs The premise fails on one definition of three, and the shape that survives is a ring on a page titled for a torus. The second axis is the one thing a torus has that a ring does not, and torus_place had already read second_axis_buys_spread=no one row over on a different question -- two readings converging on one absence.
obs The sharper half is that row 11's falsifier cannot fire in any of the 27 assignments. A falsifier that cannot fire is not a falsifier, and it was watching for a conflict these definitions are structurally incapable of having while the failure they did have went unwatched.
obs This is the lane's first reading of ITSELF, and the cheapest possible moment to take it: before the paper rather than in it.
